{"reference":"Mark 2","book":"Mark","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"Household Space in Capernaum","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Excavation at lakeside Capernaum has exposed dense domestic construction from the early Roman settlement beneath later building phases. This offers a local comparison for the crowded house, roof access, shared courtyards, and household movement in the opening scene; the description is not simply background scenery.","verse_refs":["Mark 2:1-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_002_6c12182932623a4c4633ba3f"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Healing and Dispute","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The healing account portrays embodied need and restored participation, but it should not be read as equating bodily difference with sin, weak faith, or spiritual failure. Likewise, the Sabbath disagreement belongs within Jewish arguments shaped by Jewish Scripture; it is not evidence that Judaism was uniquely legalistic.","verse_refs":["Mark 2:1-12","Mark 2:23-28"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_002_311cf3e4bb11f89f18ab396d"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why the Setting Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The chapter places healing, teaching, hospitality, crowds, and conflict in village houses and along local routes. Domestic space and village networks are therefore integral to the activity Mark narrates, rather than incidental locations.","verse_refs":["Mark 2:1-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_mark_002_4b5a286d5add06e92db0d5fe"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}